---
term: Enemy
section: building-blocks
order: 45
definition: A party in conflict with a charter where no amendment that the charter's mechanism allows would permit an arbitrated reconciliation that moves the party to neutral or associate status, short of dissolving the charter.
example: An emancipation charter holds jurisdiction over a region where some people hold slaves. No amendment that the charter allows can reconcile slaveholding with its purpose without dissolving the charter. So slave owners are its enemies, and ending their slaveholding is one of the charter's set objectives.
seeAlso:
  - status
  - adversary
  - limit-of-arbitration
---
A charter includes its enemies in its set objectives.

Carl Schmitt made the distinction between friend and enemy the defining feature of politics. Chantal Mouffe narrowed it: an enemy is a party whose conflict can't be contained, while an [[adversary]] is one whose conflict can. The enemy test gives that line a definite criterion. The enemy test marks the [[limit of arbitration]] for a single charter, through the range that the charter's own amendment mechanism can reach.

The test depends on a party's position, not its identity. A slave owner who frees their slaves leaves the position that made them an enemy, and can become a [[neutral]].

Because entrenched articles narrow the range of allowed amendments, a charter that entrenches more has more enemies. A designer can see that cost in advance.

The charter assigns enemy status, so a [[vanguard interest|vanguard]] could misuse it. But the test has a definite answer: either an allowed amendment can reach reconciliation or none can. A [[fidelity audit]] or an arbitrating parent can check a designation against the charter's own amendment rules.
